            <ArticleTitle>Metastatic carcinoma related long segment thoracic spinal epidural hematoma: a case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>E266-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/BRS.0b013e318197164f</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case report.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To report a long segment epidural hematoma with thoracic cord compression caused by a metastatic carcinoma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">To our knowledge, there have been no previous reports of spontaneous spinal epidural hematoma caused by metastatic carcinoma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 60-year-old woman with back pain and progressive leg weakness suddenly developed complete paraplegia and anesthesia below T8. A thoracic spine MRI showed an epidural hematoma and metastatic tumor. We describe the clinical course, radiographic imaging, operative findings, and treatment results.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Despite immediate surgery, the patient failed to have any significant improvement in her neurologic symptoms. A previously unsuspected primary lung tumor was diagnosed. She died 6 months after spine surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Spinal epidural hematoma can occur in association with metastatic tumors in that area.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
